on this international day of persons  with disabilities we are taking a moment  to mark and celebrate the world's  biggest minority group about 15 of the  global population that's about 1 billion  people are classified as disabled  persons according to the world health  organization that's right scarlet and  that figure is rising steadily as a  result of population growth and medical  advances disabled people are living long  healthy lives working and of course  contributing to all parts of society but  there's still a big gap in tech advances  that allow better access to everyday  things like the internet our next guest  is a lawyer philanthropist and advocate  for persons with disabilities josh basil  is the community relations manager for  accessibility it's a group making the  internet more accessible to everyone and  josh is also a disabled person living  strongly as a c45 quadriplegic josh  welcome to quick take scarlet and i are  really excited to unpack this briefly  explain to us how the internet is  inaccessible to people living with  disabilities  madison and scholar it's great to be  here today  so with the internet there's many layers  to making a website accessible  and basically when you're on a site you  have to experience it in a certain way  whether it's navigating  from the the drop down menus to  accessing pictures to the text  you name it so  what  one of the things that people don't  really know about the internet is less  than two percent of websites on the  internet actually meet accessibility  guidelines to allow persons with  disabilities to fully access websites  just like everyone else  so let's back up for a moment here josh  because it's important that we're not  only talking about people with visible  disabilities there's clearly a lot of  disabilities that are not overt that you  can't see with the naked eye so can you  clarify for us and give us a sense of  some of the invisible disabilities and  what that entails  so with invisible disabilities there's  persons with cognitive disabilities  there's there's persons with epilepsy  that if you have a light or a thing  that's blinking it can trigger the event  for somebody that goes on your website  um i'm i'm a quadriplegic i'm paralyzed  below my shoulders so a lot of times  like even doing an interview like this  today you have no idea that i'm in a  power wheelchair at the moment but  that i i operate my wheelchair with  with different technologies i operate my  computer with different technologies and  that's that's how i'm able to access the  world  what's the biggest contributor to this  problem i'm curious is it a tech problem  we don't have the innovation there is it  a willingness to spend to make these  websites more accessible and inclusive  or is it a diversity issue for the  people building the website where does  the problem lie  so the problem lies a lot of the times  and  over over history it's been just a lack  of awareness people just don't even know  that their websites are inaccessible and  like with the company that i work with  accessibility uh we have accessory.com  ace you can go put your website in and  it can test do an audit and let you know  where you're succeeding and where you're  not and that's that's one big thing is  about awareness other things over the  years  technology just hasn't been there yet  and basically the only way to actually  make a website accessible was through  manual coding  going through each line every page and  but as technology has advanced we now  have ai-powered solutions that can come  in and help do so much of what used to  only be done by hand  yeah i think that's an important point  especially as we continue to make uh new  technology more accessible i wonder  we talk a lot about the likes of  facebook now meta  it's tempting to and easy to blame a  company like facebook um  and the responsibilities that big tech  companies  face and bear for perhaps not making  their platforms as accessible as they  could be  how much do you think they deserve the  scrutiny especially as they build out  new worlds like the metaverse  so as they build out new worlds that's a  very important point you just made there  so what's what's wrong and what's been  really difficult as time go on and it's  especially true with the internet itself  persons with disabilities will not we're  not really invited to have a seat at the  table in the beginning days and as it  grew less and less accessible it become  basically persons with disabilities  became an afterthought and with  diversity equity inclusion being put  more into corporations all across the  world people are talking about uh making  sure that that diversity equity and  inclusion really is has a part within  the business world we're starting to  have better conversations so if the  metaverse is built and persons with  disabilities persons with diversity  are not  included then we're going to have a huge  problem but if they are included from  the beginning then we can do this right  and  we're not going to have the same problem  of having less than two percent of the  net being accessible uh i'm excited  about the metaverse it's it makes me  think about like ready player one and  being able to like really immerse  yourself in a new  kind of world of however you want to  draw up that reality which really really  excites me but i  just love to be a part of it from the  beginning and and so if that's the case  and i know video game companies are kind  of at the forefront of all this how are  the activation blizzards or the road  boxes which are platforms of course are  they leaders or are they followers in  this regard  um  i don't know if i want to speak to those  unique companies but it's just  i know we all can do better and what it  takes is making sure that persons with  disabilities are hired within your  businesses  giving them a seat at the table hearing  their unique perspectives persons with  disabilities are so  they're have so many talents so many  skills they're natural problem solvers  every single day we have to overcome  barriers so i just i love the idea of  having these type of corporations bring  in more people that have expertise in  this area bringing in more consultants  but making sure that you do it right  from the beginning and if you do that  you start creating a disability  inclusion culture where it's ingrained  within the corporation's dna and like i  said before it then doesn't become an  afterthought  you know we started this conversation  with the statistic that disabled people  are the world's largest minority group  making up 15 of the global population  that's a huge chunk of individuals to  not have access equitable access to  something as  big as the internet obviously you know  why are we still not doing enough to  include and center differently abled  people particularly uh in the workplace  well first of all  by you guys today having this  conversation you're doing so much good  to make sure that we bring this  awareness to the world and then when it  comes to employment the trouble is is  that if you don't have people within the  employment world  with disabilities you're not you're not  hearing from them you're not seeing them  you're not  having their ability for them to  contribute especially at all levels  whether it's at the lower level of a  company or upper management as as  someone rises in their career so the  more people that we have with  disabilities within all parts of of of  life we're going to see so much of a  different world we're gonna have  different conversations and um the  persons with disabilities right now are  the most unemployed and underemployed  population in the entire world they've  been really excluded they haven't been  there hasn't been an inclusive hiring  process within most businesses they just  they haven't taken the time to learn  kind of what what it is and what it what  you can do to better hire and find  talent within the disability community  right right and as i'm hearing you talk  about that it's clear that you know it's  a diverse community it's not a monolith  of course but i am curious if you see  any big overarching theme for for the  biggest thing that employers get wrong  about serving their disabled employees  you know when we think about ada  guidelines we maybe think about  something like ramps or things like that  infrastructure is there uh any other  kind of pushes that would be better for  employers or in addition for employers  to focus on  so the big thing is to not make  assumptions so a lot of times you would  look at me  i'm paralyzed i have a power wheelchair  obviously you can't see it right now but  you'd think i'm very limited in certain  things that i can do  like through technology through  all these different things that are  available to me allow me to get my mind  my voice into the world i graduated  magna [ __ ] laude from law school without  ever flipping a page with my fingers  a lot of people probably didn't think  that would be possible but it's like  it's just understanding that  not to assume somebody can or cannot do  something give them opportunity have the  right conversation and you know you're  going to see incredible things happen  within your corporation and i think so  many small businesses just need to look  look at all your employees do you have  to do you have diversity do you have  disability making up all facets of your  business and then even with small  businesses making sure that getting your  website successful like accessory  provides an ai powered solution that  allows you to do it and it's it's  incredible to be able to bring these  type of technologies that can then scale  it from that two percent to five percent  the 50 of the internet which i cannot  wait for the day without the internet's  more accessible to people  i think that's a beautiful place to  leave it josh really appreciate you  joining us and sharing all of that  information thank you so much josh basil  is an activist for the disabled and  community as a whole and community  relations manager for accessibility

